<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sámos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samos is a Greek island in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>, separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> by the Mycale Strait. In ancient times it was a rich and powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>, known for its vineyards and wine, and it reached the height of its prosperity under the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a> in the 6th century BCE, when the engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eupalinos">Eupalinos</a> dug a tunnel through a mountain to bring water to the city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos">Heraion of Samos</a>, a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunnel-of-eupalinos">Eupalinian aqueduct</a> are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epicurus">Epicurus</a> and the astronomer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristarchus-of-samos">Aristarchus of Samos</a> were born there. From 1835 the island was a semi-autonomous principality under Ottoman suzerainty, until it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 1913.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samos.jpg" alt="Samos" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pe-sa · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samos_049_2009.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
